toml++: report malformed values that reach the parser's assertions instead of assuming they can't happen

Each change replaces a TOML_ASSERT_ASSUME in parse_value() or the date/time parsers that malformed
input can break, or that only the caller's dispatch keeps true. Debug builds abort on the assert();
NDEBUG builds turn it into __builtin_assume(), which is undefined behaviour. With the patch,
toml::parse throws toml::parse_error (or returns a failed parse_result without exceptions), as it
does for any other malformed input.

1. TaskSmack #1388: parse_value() opened with TOML_ASSERT_ASSUME(!is_value_terminator(*cp)), but
   the cursor can sit on a terminator there: the '}' in 'm=[}' (an array closed with the wrong
   bracket). This raises the parser's normal error instead, with the same message the key-value
   path already uses ("expected value, saw '}'").

2. TaskSmack #1659: parse_value()'s scan() lambda asserted the same thing on entry. A date
   followed by a space and a digit ('YYYY-MM-DD 1', possibly a space-separated date-time) re-runs
   scan() one character past that digit, which can be a terminator: the second ' ' in
   'h=2000-02-1a 1 1', the newline in 'h=2000-02-01 1\n', the ']' in 'h=[2000-02-01 1]'. scan()
   now returns without scanning, as it already did at end-of-file, so the caller backs up to the
   10-character date and parses only that. The date parser rejects '2000-02-1a', and a valid date
   leaves ' 1 ...' behind, which the key-value and array paths reject.

3. TaskSmack #2427: parse_time() asserted that it starts on a digit. parse_value() picks
   parse_date_time() from the traits of the whole token (a '-', a ':' and a 't'/'T' anywhere in
   it), so nothing checks the character after the 'T'/'t' separator, and parse_date_time() hands
   it straight to parse_time(): the 'h' in 'h=0200-02-10thexxxxxxxxxxWq\x7f:t', the 'x' in
   'a=2000-02-10Tx:', the '+' in 'a=2000-02-10T+1:'. parse_time() now reports "expected 2-digit
   hour". parse_date() and parse_date_time() made the same assumption; parse_value()'s dispatch
   does guarantee a digit there today, but they now report "expected 4-digit year" instead of
   assuming it, so a future caller can't turn a malformed value into UB. The rest of the date,
   time and offset parsing already checks every character through consume_digit_sequence().

Base: marzer/tomlplusplus 1e8829b793b66ad17011732a146b8077d379b011 (the GIT_TAG in
cmake/Dependencies.cmake). Patches the split headers under include/ (what TaskSmack compiles
against) and the generated single header toml.hpp alike. Applied by FetchContent's PATCH_COMMAND
through cmake/patches/ApplyPatch.cmake, which skips it when it is already applied. Drop this file
once upstream fixes all of these assertions (still present in upstream master, 1e8829b, as of
2026-10-17).

